  dishSprite: {fileID: -8053197629789734714, guid: 46112b9472351694db800b4770b84eac, type: 3}
  hDialogLines:
  - text: "Alright! Today, we\u2019re starting with one of the most iconic dishes
      in Filipino cuisine "
  - text: But before we start cooking, let me tell you about the History of Adobo
  - text: "Acording to De la Cruz, E. (2025, July 16). A brief history of adobo,
      the Philippines\u2019 national dish."
  - text: Adobo in the Philippines grew out of indigenous preservation and cooking
      practices that used vinegar (and other souring agents), salt, and garlic,
  - text: "to preserve meat and flavor food long before Spanish contact. When the
      Spanish arrived they applied the word adobo (from adobar, \u201Cto marinate\u201D)"
  - text: to these native preparations early colonial records (17th-century vocabularies)
      refer to adobo-style dishes as adobo de los naturales. And over time, soy sauce
      was introduced, blending both local and foreign flavors
  - text: Over time adobo absorbed external influences (for example, soy sauce from
      Chinese trade), producing the many regional and household variants like white
      adobo, adobo sa gata, adobong baboy, adobong manok, etc
  - text: "It\u2019s simple, delicious, and truly Filipino at heart!"
  - text: "And becoming a ubiquitous, long-shelf-life staple often considered the
      Philippines\u2019 unofficial national dish."
  - text: 'Now lets start '
